2. cognitive load
    -good user experience requires low brain usage
    -more cognitive load 

3. Doherty threshold
    -if any user experience is taking more than 400ms, it annoys users
    -preloading so the apps are quick

4. Fitts Law
    -The closer things are, the quicker we get it.
    -The close the items, the quicker the people notice it
    -Make small objects Larger

5. Goal Gradient Effect
    -The tendency to work harder as one gets closer to the goal

6. Hick's Law
    -The time to make a decision increase with the number and complexity of choices
    -The more options you provide, the more complex the experience

7. Jakob's Law
    -Common user design align with good user experience

8. Law of Common Region
    -Defining boundaries helps user recognize related elements

9. Law of Proximity
    -We can group items based on proximity, and user will think its one part

10. Law of Pragnanz
    -Design should aim for simplicity to be easily understood

11. Law of Similarity
    -use similarity in color, shape, or size to create visual grouping
    
12. Law of Uniforn Connectedness
    -Elements that are visually connected are perceived as related.

13. Mental Model
    -User form mental models of how systems work based on their experiences

14. Miller's Law
    -Average person can keep 7+-2 items in their working directory
    -Limit the number of items presented to avoid overwhelming users
    -Bring everything into single memory click, dont make users remember previous interaction.

15. Occam's Razor
    -Opt of simplest solutions

16. Paradox of the Active User
    -Users start using software immediately and rarely read manuals
    -similar to 80/20 
        -only 20% of the items will be used 80% of the time.

17. 

18. Parkinson's Law
    -15 minute survey, but user will think it is 7 minute survey
        -user will get annoyed if survey takes more than 7 minutes
    -Design the user interface based on this idea

19. Peak End Rule
    -Focus on creating memorable high points and strong finish
    -How am I rewarding the users, so they remember it as good experience

20. Postel's Law
    -Be liberal in what you accept, be conservative in what you send
    -A form input that accepts various inputs, and provides clear errors

21. Selective Attention
    -Give users selective attention, why it is not working
    -give specific reasons, dont let users guess

22. Serial Position Effect
    -Users remember the first and last items in a series best.
    -Identify if primacy or recency is more suitable

23. Tesler's Law
    -A certain amount of complexity is inherent in any system
    -Dont eliminate, try to present in a simpler way.

24. Von Restorff Effect
    -Distinctive items standout and are easier to remember
    -If there is something that should inform the user, do a notif

25. Zerigarnik Effect
    -People get anxious about incompleted tasks


